theory.intro:
The direction of a nonzero vector is usually given as its **angle of inclination**, the angle turned anticlockwise from the positive $x$-axis, with $0^{\circ}\leq\theta<360^{\circ}$. Use degree mode. Sketch the vector; when both components are nonzero, find the reference angle $A=\tan^{-1}\frac{|y|}{|x|}$, then let the quadrant fix $\theta$. Handle axis directions directly; the zero vector has no direction.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta,angles,quotes,calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (-2.4,1.8);
\coordinate (W) at (-3,0);
\coordinate (E) at (3,0);
\coordinate (F) at (-2.4,0);
\draw[->] (-3.2,0)--(3.2,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.8)--(0,2.8) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[dashed] (P)--(F);
\draw[-{Stealth},thick] (O)--(P) node[above right]{$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\pic[draw, "$A$", angle radius=0.6cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle = P--O--W};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=1.2cm, angle eccentricity=1.45] {angle = E--O--P};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
Here $x<0$ and $y>0$, so the vector lies in the second quadrant.
$A=\tan^{-1}\frac{3}{4}$
$=36.87^{\circ}$
$\theta=180^{\circ}-36.87^{\circ}$
$\approx 143^{\circ}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta,angles,quotes,calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (-2.4,1.8);
\coordinate (W) at (-3,0);
\coordinate (F) at (-2.4,0);
\draw[->] (-3.2,0)--(3.2,0) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.8)--(0,2.8) node[above]{$y$};
\draw[dashed] (P)--(F);
\draw[-{Stealth},thick] (O)--(P) node[above right]{$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\pic[draw, "$A$", angle radius=0.6cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle = P--O--W};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Both components are positive, so the vector lies in the first quadrant.
$A=\tan^{-1}\frac{4}{4}$
$=45^{\circ}$
$\theta=45^{\circ}$
$\text{bearing}=90^{\circ}-45^{\circ}$
$=045^{\circ}\text{T}$
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.4]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta, angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Npt) at (0,2.8);
\coordinate (A) at (2,2);
\draw[->] (-0.6,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$E$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6) -- (Npt) node[above] {$N$};
\draw[-{Stealth},thick] (O) -- (A) node[above right] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\pic[draw, "$45^{\circ}$", angle radius=0.9cm, angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle = A--O--Npt};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

mastery.question_text:
A ferry's displacement from the harbour is $\underset{\sim}{s}=-7\underset{\sim}{i}+24\underset{\sim}{j}$, measured in kilometres, with $\underset{\sim}{i}$ east and $\underset{\sim}{j}$ north.
Find the true bearing of the ferry from the harbour, to the nearest degree.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[->] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right]{$E$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.3) node[above]{$N$};
\draw[dashed] (-1.05,3.6)--(-1.05,0);
\draw[-{Stealth},thick] (0,0)--(-1.05,3.6) node[above left]{$\underset{\sim}{s}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
